Although its origins were in unfinished demo compositions like "Don't Be Crazy" and "My Life", it was one of the last songs to be completed in time for the ''Double Fantasy'' sessions. "We didn't hear it until the last day of rehearsal," producer [[Jack Douglas (record producer)|Jack Douglas]] said in 2005.<ref>"Just Like Starting Over" by [[Chris Hunt]], ''[[Uncut (magazine)|Uncut]]'' John Lennon Special, 2005</ref> Lennon finished the song while on holiday in [[Bermuda]], and recorded it at [[The Hit Factory]] in New York City just weeks later. The song was originally titled "Starting Over"; however, "(Just Like)" was added prior to its release because of its similarity to [[Dolly Parton]]'s "Starting Over Again" which had topped the [[Hot Country Songs|US Country Charts]] earlier in the year.  The chiming bell that opens the song was a deliberate allusion to the heavy tolling church bell that opens Lennon's 1970 song "[[Mother (John Lennon song)|Mother]]", illustrating how far Lennon had come in ten years.<ref>[https://twitter.com/johnlennon/status/1579193485432745986?lang=en Official Tweet] from The John Lennon Estate</ref><ref>[https://ultimateclassicrock.com/john-lennon-final-charttopper-starting-over/ How Fate Intervened on John Lennon's Last Chart-Topping Single], Ultimate Classic Rock</ref>

While commercial releases of the song (original 45&nbsp;rpm singles, LPs and compact discs) run a length of three minutes and 54 seconds, a promotional 12-inch vinyl single originally issued to radio stations features a longer fadeout, officially running at four minutes 17 seconds.{{cn|date=December 2021}}

Musicologist [[Walter Everett (musicologist)|Walter Everett]] noted melodic similarities between a portion of the song and [[the Beach Boys]]' 1964 single "[[Don't Worry Baby]]".<ref name="Chair2008">{{cite book|last=Everett|first=Walter|author-link=Walter Everett (musicologist)|title=The Foundations of Rock : From "Blue Suede Shoes" to "Suite: Judy Blue Eyes"|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=p0p6jL-u3T4C&pg=PT200|year=2008|publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]|isbn=978-0-19-971870-2|page=200}}</ref>

